def u(i,p,v,why='Align the assessment with the verified protocol conditions.'):q(n,i,p,v,why)
def o(i,j,k,v):u(i,['options',j,k],v)
u(1,['prompt'],'`bob.example` publishes `MX 20 mxa.bob.example`, `MX 10 mxb.bob.example` and `MX 10 mxc.bob.example`. With no special routing policy and ordinary connection-failure fallback, which destination preference is correct?')
o(1,1,'explanation','This ignores the published preference ordering. Parallel connection attempts and duplicate message delivery are different issues.')
o(1,2,'explanation','Lower preference is tried first. Equal-preference destinations are randomised unless the sender has a reason to favour one; this spreads attempts without guaranteeing even load.')
u(1,['workedExample'],'1. Order destinations by preference: 10 (mxb, mxc), then 20 (mxa).\n2. Randomise the equal-preference group absent a reason to favour one.\n3. Connection failure can lead to trying another destination.\n4. Temporary delivery failure can lead to queuing and later retry.\n5. A permanent recipient rejection should not be bypassed simply by trying a backup.')
o(2,1,'explanation','An immediate bounce is inappropriate for this temporary recipient failure. A later queue expiration can still cause a failure notification; bounces are not limited to 5xx replies.')
u(2,['workedExample'],'1. The first digit 4 indicates a transient failure.\n2. Preserve the failed recipient delivery for retry rather than retrying in a tight loop.\n3. RFC 5321 recommends a give-up time generally at least 4–5 days; retry schedules depend on policy.\n4. Queue expiration can generate a failure notification for previously accepted mail, unless the reverse path is null.\n\nGreylisting relies on retries, but retrying is not proof that a sender is legitimate.')
o(3,1,'explanation','In this example Alice\'s provider delivers separately to Carol\'s domain; Bob\'s server is not responsible for forwarding that copy.')
u(4,['prompt'],json.loads((r/(n+'.questions.json')).read_text())[3]['prompt']+' Assume both domains have organisational domain `shop.example`.')
u(5,['prompt'],'Bob forwards mail to Gmail without altering the envelope sender or signed content. Alice\'s SPF record ends in `-all` and does not authorise the university\'s IP. Alice\'s aligned DKIM signature remains valid at Gmail, and her DMARC policy is `p=reject`. What authentication result follows?')
o(5,2,'explanation','SPF fails under the stated authorisation conditions, but the still-valid aligned DKIM signature suffices for DMARC.')
u(5,['workedExample'],'At Gmail, SPF fails because the university IP is not authorised for the preserved envelope sender. DKIM passes and aligns, so DMARC passes. This does not guarantee inbox delivery.\n\nModifying a signed subject or covered body content could instead break DKIM. A footer outside an optional `l=` signed prefix is not necessarily covered, and an unsigned subject is a different case.')
u(6,['prompt'],'In a hypothetical SPF evaluation, no early mechanism matches. Five top-level `include` terms and four nested DNS-query-causing terms are evaluated, followed by `mx` and `a`. All other DNS responses and syntax are valid. Why does the evaluation return `permerror`?')
o(6,0,'explanation','Multiple includes are permitted. The budget counts evaluated DNS-query-causing terms across recursive evaluation.')
o(6,1,'text','It attempts an eleventh DNS-query-causing term, exceeding the ten-term limit')
o(6,1,'explanation','RFC 7208 section 4.6.4 limits these evaluated terms to ten. This is not a limit of ten individual DNS queries or packets.')
u(6,['workedExample'],'The running count is five outer includes plus four nested terms = nine; `mx` is ten and the attempted `a` is eleven, producing permerror. An earlier matching mechanism would have stopped evaluation sooner.\n\nRemove unnecessary authorised senders or separate sending domains appropriately. Flattening dynamic provider policies into static IP lists requires reliable updates. An SPF permerror does not supply a DMARC pass; a valid aligned DKIM signature could still do so.')
u(7,['workedExample'],'Within the server/account, the cache identity includes mailbox, UIDVALIDITY and UID. A changed UIDVALIDITY means UID 4827 cannot be assumed to identify the old message. Invalidate old UID mappings and resynchronise the current mailbox. This does not require destroying unrelated locally saved copies. Mutable flags require updates even when the UID identity remains valid.')
o(8,0,'text','Without a mandatory authenticated-TLS policy, stripping an opportunistic STARTTLS offer can induce plaintext SMTP relay')
o(8,1,'explanation','An enforce-mode policy requires authenticated TLS to approved MX hosts. Before a policy is cached, an attacker can suppress DNS discovery; HTTPS alone does not remove that first-contact vulnerability.')
u(8,['workedExample'],'TLS provides transport confidentiality and integrity; peer authentication requires certificate/identity validation. STARTTLS can be downgraded when the sender permits plaintext. Effective MTA-STS enforce policies and DNSSEC-validated DANE records constrain delivery, with different discovery assumptions. Port 465 starts TLS before SMTP. SPF checks sender authorisation, DKIM checks a signed domain/content association, and DMARC checks alignment; none encrypts the message.')
o(9,1,'explanation','Do not authorise unidentified sources merely to clear failures. Geography and authentication failure alone do not prove spoofing: investigate ownership and legitimate sending paths.')
o(9,3,'text','Give the invoicer a valid aligned DKIM signature or an aligned custom return path with SPF authorising its servers, and investigate unidentified sources')
o(9,3,'explanation','At least one passing mechanism must align. Merely changing the return-path domain without SPF authorisation is insufficient; DKIM can survive forwarding when its signature remains valid.')
u(9,['workedExample'],'Microsoft 365 already supplies an aligned pass. The invoicer authenticates only invoicer.example, which is unaligned with corp.example, so its messages can be rejected under enforcement. Configure and verify DKIM using d=corp.example and/or an aligned return-path domain whose SPF authorises the sending servers. Investigate unknown sources before classifying them; their country does not establish spoofing. Monitor reports and delivery failures during rollout.')
o(10,3,'explanation','The framing disagreement described establishes a message-injection/spoofing path; it does not by itself establish a crash.')
u(10,['workedExample'],'An attacker places a nonstandard end-of-data sequence followed by SMTP commands in content accepted by provider A. A relays one message, but vulnerable receiver B treats the sequence as the end and parses a second transaction. If A\'s IP is authorised for the forged envelope domain and that domain aligns with the forged From domain, SPF can provide a DMARC pass. This depends on the particular parser pair and authorisation conditions; it does not decrypt TLS or grant mailbox access.')
u(11,['prompt'],'A mailing list receives a valid aligned DKIM-signed message from `alice@alice.example` (DMARC `p=reject`). The signature covers Subject and the full body. The list changes both, keeps Alice\'s From header, and uses its own unaligned envelope domain, with no other aligned signature. Which statements are true? Select all that apply.')
o(11,1,'text','SPF is evaluated for the list\'s envelope domain; even a pass for that unaligned domain does not provide a DMARC pass')
o(11,1,'explanation','The preserved visible From domain is alice.example, which does not align with the list\'s stated envelope domain.')
u(11,['workedExample'],'The subject and covered body modifications invalidate Alice\'s signature. SPF for the list\'s unaligned envelope domain cannot supply alignment. DMARC therefore fails; a receiver may apply p=reject or use local policy, including trusted ARC evidence, to override disposition. Alternatives include preserving the signed content or changing From to an appropriately authenticated list identity. An ARC chain is not an automatic exemption.')
